.TH COMPILE 1 "October 11, 2026" "Vircon32 DevTools 26.04.24" "Vircon32 Development Tools"
.SH NAME
compile \- Vircon32 C compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B compile
.RB [ \-o
.IR output.asm ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-g ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-\-debugmode ]
.I input.c
.br
.B compile
.B \-\-help
.br
.B compile
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B compile
translates one C source file, written in the Vircon32 dialect of C
(see
.BR LANGUAGE ,
below), into Vircon32 assembly. The assembly is then turned into a
machine-code binary by
.BR assemble (1),
and the binary is packed together with its textures and sounds into a
cartridge by
.BR packrom (1).
.PP
There is no linker. A Vircon32 program is always a single translation
unit: every other source file it needs, including the standard
library, is brought in with
.BR #include .
The standard library headers
.RB ( video.h ", " input.h ", " audio.h ", " time.h ,
.BR math.h ", " string.h ", " misc.h ", " memcard.h )
contain the full definitions of their functions, not just
declarations; only the functions a program actually uses are emitted.
See
.BR vircon32 (3v32).
.PP
Compilation runs in five stages: lexer, preprocessor, parser, semantic
analyzer and emitter. Each stage stops the compilation if it found any
error. Nothing is printed on success unless
.B \-v
is given.
.PP
Exactly one input file is accepted. Options and the input file may
appear in any order.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output.asm"
Write the assembly to
.IR output.asm .
Without
.BR \-o ,
the output path is the input path with its extension replaced by
.IR .asm ,
so
.I game.c
becomes
.IR game.asm .
.TP
.B \-b
Compile the program as a BIOS instead of a cartridge program. Besides
.BR main ,
a BIOS must define
.BR "void error_handler()" ,
which the console runs when the CPU raises a hardware error, and its
code is placed at the BIOS program ROM address
.RB ( 0x10000000 )
instead of the cartridge one
.RB ( 0x20000000 ).
Ordinary games never need this.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose: report the output path chosen (when
.B \-o
was not given), each compilation stage as it starts, and the final
result.
.TP
.B \-g
Also write a debug information file,
.IR output.asm.debug ,
which relates the generated assembly to the C source: source file and
line for each statement, and the functions they belong to. It is meant
for debuggers and profilers, not for people.
.TP
.B \-w
Inhibit all warnings.
.TP
.B \-\-debugmode
Write the results of the internal stages next to the output file:
.I output.asm.lexer.log
(the token stream after preprocessing) and
.I output.asm.parser.log
(the syntax tree, as XML). Intended for debugging the compiler itself.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the compiler version and author, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-Wall ", " \-c ", " \-s ", " \-O1 ", " \-O2 ", " \-O3
Accepted for compatibility with other compilers and build scripts, but
they have no effect. In particular there is no optimizer; see
.BR v32opt (1)
for an external one.
.PP
Any other argument that starts with
.B \-
is an error.
.SH LANGUAGE
The compiler accepts a C dialect that is close to C89/C99 but differs
from it in many places. The most important differences:
.SS Data types
.IP \(bu 2
The machine is word-addressed: every scalar type is one 32-bit word,
and
.B sizeof
counts words.
.BR char ,
.B short
and
.B long
are accepted but silently become
.BR int ,
and
.B double
becomes
.BR float .
There are no
.BR unsigned ,
.BR signed ,
.BR static ,
.B volatile
or
.B register
keywords.
.IP \(bu 2
The types are
.BR void ,
.B bool
(with literals
.B true
and
.BR false ),
.BR int ,
.BR float ,
pointers, arrays,
.BR struct ,
.B union
and
.BR enum ,
plus
.BR typedef .
A string is an array of words, one character per word, terminated by
0; string literals have type
.BR int* .
.IP \(bu 2
Arrays are declared with the size before the name:
.RS
.IP
.nf
int[ 10 ] scores;
int[ 4 ][ 8 ] grid;
.fi
.RE
.IP
The standard spelling
.I int scores[10]
is rejected with a message saying so. Sizes may be constant expressions
(macros, enum constants, arithmetic).
.IP \(bu 2
Function pointer types put the parameter list before the
.BR * :
.RS
.IP
.nf
int( int, int )* operation;
operation = &add;
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
.B struct
appears only in a structure's definition or forward declaration
.RI ( "struct Node;" ).
Everywhere else the structure is named on its own:
.IR "Node* next;" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B NULL
is a keyword, and its value is \-1, not 0. A pointer cannot be
assigned the integer 0; use
.BR NULL .
.SS Functions
.IP \(bu 2
The program's entry point is
.BR "void main()" ;
it takes no arguments and returns nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
Every parameter and every return value must be exactly one word. A
structure, union or array larger than that cannot be passed or
returned by value; pass a pointer instead.
.IP \(bu 2
A prototype must name its parameters
.RI ( "void move( int dx, int dy );" ),
and every function declared must also be defined somewhere in the
translation unit.
.SS Statements and expressions
.IP \(bu 2
There is no conditional operator
.RB ( ?: )
and no comma operator.
.IP \(bu 2
A variable cannot be declared directly in a
.B switch
body after the first
.B case
or
.B default
label (inside a nested block it can).
.IP \(bu 2
Float literals have no exponent form
.RI ( 1e5
is an error) and need a digit before the point
.RI ( 0.5 ,
not
.IR .5 ).
.IP \(bu 2
The escapes accepted are
.BR \en ", " \er ", " \et ", " \e\e ", " \e\(aq ", " \e\(dq
and
.BI \ex HH
with exactly two hex digits. There are no octal escapes and no
.BR \e0 ;
write
.B \ex00
instead.
.IP \(bu 2
Right shift of a negative value does not extend the sign.
.IP \(bu 2
A compound assignment
.RI ( "a[i++] += 1" )
evaluates its target expression twice.
.IP \(bu 2
An array converts to a pointer only when assigned, used as an
initializer, or passed as an argument; as an operand of
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.B ==
or unary
.B *
it is an error. Take
.I &array[0]
explicitly.
.IP \(bu 2
Reading a
.B const
value into a plain variable by assignment, as an argument or as a
return value is rejected ("discards const qualifier"); an initializer,
or an explicit cast to the same type, is accepted.
.SS Global data
.IP \(bu 2
Global variables live in RAM and are initialized when the program
starts.
.B extern
declares a global that is fully defined elsewhere in the same
translation unit (there is no other kind).
.IP \(bu 2
.B embedded
places the contents of a binary file in the cartridge program ROM and
names it as a read-only global:
.RS
.IP
.nf
embedded int[ 64 ][ 48 ] level1 = "maps/level1.vmap";
.fi
.RE
.IP
It is allowed only at file scope. The file is read by
.BR assemble (1),
relative to the directory the assembler is run from, and its size must
be a multiple of 4 bytes. Tile maps made by
.BR tiled2vircon (1)
are the usual use.
.SS Inline assembly
An
.B asm
block holds assembly lines, one string literal per line, which are
copied into the output. A variable of one word can be named inside a
line in braces; the compiler substitutes its location:
.PP
.RS
.nf
int get_frame()
{
    asm
    {
        "in R0, TIM_FrameCounter"
    }
}

void set_x( int x )
{
    asm
    {
        "mov R0, {x}"
        "out GPU_DrawingPointX, R0"
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A function's return value is whatever is in
.B R0
when it returns, so an
.B asm
block that leaves its result in
.B R0
needs no
.B return
statement. Registers other than
.B R0
must be preserved
.RB ( push / pop ).
See
.BR assemble (1)
for the assembly language.
.SH PREPROCESSOR
Supported directives:
.BR #include ,
.BR #define ,
.BR #undef ,
.BR #ifdef ,
.BR #ifndef ,
.BR #else ,
.BR #endif ,
.B #error
and
.BR #warning .
A backslash at the end of a line continues it.
.PP
.B #define
creates object-like macros and function-like macros with arguments. A
macro body cannot contain the
.B #
character, so there is no stringizing or token pasting, and an
object-like macro cannot refer to itself.
.PP
There is no
.BR #if ,
.BR #elif ,
.BR #pragma ,
.BR #line ,
and no
.BR defined() ;
any other directive is an error.
.PP
Only the quoted form of
.B #include
exists:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "video.h"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The angle-bracket form
.RI ( "#include <video.h>" )
is rejected. A file is searched for first in the directory of the file
containing the
.B #include
line, then in the
.I include
directory next to the
.B compile
executable itself, which holds the standard library headers.
Includes may nest up to 20 levels deep.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors and warnings are written to standard error as
.PP
.RS
.nf
file:line:column: error: description
file:line:column: warning: description
file:line:column: fatal error: description
.fi
.RE
.PP
A fatal error stops the compilation at once. A failed compilation ends
with a final line such as
.PP
.RS
.nf
compile: error: compilation terminated
compile: error: parser finished with errors
.fi
.RE
.PP
naming the stage that failed. The compiler stops after 15 errors. At most 10
warnings are shown; after that it reports
.I "warning: maximum warnings have been reached"
and stays quiet.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program compiled and the assembly was written (or
.BR \-\-help / \-\-version
was given).
.TP
.B 1
Any error: a bad command line, an unreadable input, or a compilation
error in any stage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build a cartridge from a C program, a texture and a sound:
.PP
.RS
.nf
compile game.c \-o obj/game.asm
assemble obj/game.asm \-o obj/game.vbin
png2vircon sprites.png \-o obj/sprites.vtex
wav2vircon jump.wav \-o obj/jump.vsnd
packrom game.xml \-o game.v32
.fi
.RE
.PP
where
.I game.xml
is the ROM definition described in
.BR packrom (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <DevTools>/include/
The standard library headers, found next to the
.B compile
executable. With the default installation
.I <DevTools>
is
.I /usr/local/Vircon32/DevTools
on Linux and macOS.
.TP
.IR output.asm.debug ", " output.asm.lexer.log ", " output.asm.parser.log
Written only with
.B \-g
and
.BR \-\-debugmode .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR assemble (1),
.BR packrom (1),
.BR disassemble (1),
.BR Vircon32 (1),
.BR vircon32 (3v32),
.BR v32c++ (1),
.BR v32opt (1)
.PP
The Vircon32 website,
.IR https://www.vircon32.com ,
has the full C language and library documentation.
.SH BUGS
.B \-Wall
is listed as enabling all warnings, but it does nothing; neither does
.BR \-c .
.PP
A file named on an
.B embedded
line is read relative to the assembler's working directory, not to the
C source file, so the program builds only from the directory its build
script expects.
.SH AUTHOR
The Vircon32 C compiler was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
This manual page was written by Matthew Haas for the v32c++ project.
